(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When is intravenous antibiotic therapy used in Lyme disease?","choices":["For some cutaneous manifestations, acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans and arthritis","For every patient at the moment the erythema migrans lesion is first recognised","For prophylaxis in anyone bitten by a tick in an endemic region of the country","For patients who cannot tolerate doxycycline, in place of a macrolide antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or penicillin G are the agents named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How does a hobo spider bite present?","choices":["An immediately painful sting with erythema, swelling and urticaria developing at the site","A slowly advancing serpentine trail that progresses two to three centimetres each day","A painless bite with induration and paraesthesia within thirty minutes, a large red area, and vesicles within thirty-six hours","A painful bite with sweating and piloerection within thirty minutes, then crampy abdominal pain and muscle spasms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — eschar may follow, and systemic symptoms include headache, nausea and memory impairment. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is characteristic of fire ant venom and behaviour?","choices":["The venom is injected through hollow hairs, and contact occurs passively on brushing","The venom is cytotoxic, producing central necrosis with a surrounding blanched rim","The venom induces mast cell degranulation, and the ants usually attack in groups","The venom is a neurotoxin, and the insects sting only when their nest is disturbed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — flushing, pruritus, hives, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea can follow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In which patients might scabies excoriations be seen along the head and neck?","choices":["Patients who have already been treated once","Infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised","Adolescents and young adults in shared housing","Pregnant patients in their third trimester"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — healthy adults have head and neck sparing. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which patients are at increased risk of complications from a black widow bite, and how is envenomation treated?","choices":["The very old, the very young and those with cardiovascular disease; treat with calcium gluconate, narcotics, muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines","Pregnant patients and children under eight years of age; treat with doxycycline given through a graded desensitisation protocol","Immunosuppressed patients and the elderly; treat with ivermectin every two weeks for three doses","Patients with a positive skin test; treat with subcutaneous epinephrine and desensitisation therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and tetanus vaccination status must be up to date. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"By which mechanisms do caterpillars produce skin disease?","choices":["Burrowing into the stratum corneum where the organism deposits its eggs and faeces","Mechanical irritation by pointed hairs, toxin injection through hollow hairs, and hypersensitivity to hairs","Direct penetration of the skin by larvae that then migrate through the epidermal layer of the skin","Injection of a neurotoxin through a modified ovipositor when the insect is threatened"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the aggregate of these effects is called lepidopterism. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In whom is body louse infestation typically found, and how does it persist?","choices":["Schoolchildren sharing hats and hair accessories, persisting through repeated close head contact","Sexually active adults across all social levels, often with a concurrent sexually transmitted infection","Long-term care residents who are elderly and immunosuppressed, persisting through staff carriage","Homeless people, refugees, war victims and those in crowded conditions, persisting via unwashed clothing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — transmission is through contaminated clothing and bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which three lice cause pediculosis, and which body regions do they affect?","choices":["Phthirus pubis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus capitis on pubic skin, and Cimex pilosellus on the trunk","Pediculus humanus capitis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus humanus on the trunk, Phthirus pubis on pubic skin","Pediculus humanus capitis on the trunk, Pediculus humanus humanus on the scalp, Phthirus pubis on the eyelashes","Sarcoptes scabiei on the scalp, Cimex lectularius on the trunk, and Phthirus pubis on pubic skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The body louse is about 30% larger than the head louse. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is hookworm folliculitis?","choices":["Pustules on an erythematous base each pierced by a single central hair shaft","Indurated crusted nodules on the trunk of an infant with heavy infestation","Follicular papules and pustules confined to one location, usually the buttock","Follicular pustules over the trunk and buttocks after hot tub exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it may need repeated courses of treatment. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which age group is most affected by head lice, and how is it usually transmitted?","choices":["Homeless adults, primarily by contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed","Infants under one year, primarily by contact with the bedding of an infested carer","Children between three and twelve years, primarily by direct head-to-head contact","Adults over forty years, primarily by sexual contact and shared towels or bedding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Indirect spread through combs, brushes, bedding and headgear is less common. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which organism causes Lyme disease, and how is it maintained in the environment?","choices":["Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete, maintained by transstadial passage through tick life stages with birds and deer as dispersal animals","Rickettsia rickettsii, a bacterium, maintained in dog ticks, wood ticks and rodents across the southeastern and south central states","Sarcoptes scabiei, maintained by close contact between humans in crowded living conditions","A parasitic flatworm, maintained in snails and waterfowl in fresh water and released as cercariae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it occurs across the northern hemisphere in North America, Europe and Asia. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Once a reaction pattern has been recognised, how is the differential narrowed further?","choices":["By the duration of the eruption, the patient's age, and whether treatment has been tried","By culture and sensitivity of the lesion together with a complete blood count","By whether the patient reports pruritus and whether there is regional lymphadenopathy","By colour, shape, configuration of lesions relative to one another, and distribution on the body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those characteristics tune the differential the reaction pattern generated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How is Rocky Mountain spotted fever treated, including in pregnancy and young children?","choices":["Intravenous ceftriaxone for all patients, with oral therapy reserved for prophylaxis","Doxycycline for adults, pregnant patients and children, with desensitisation where it is contraindicated","Amoxicillin for children and pregnant patients, with doxycycline reserved for adults only","Azithromycin for all patients, since doxycycline is contraindicated below eight years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 100 mg twice daily for adults and 2.2 mg per kilogram twice daily for children, for 5 to 10 days. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which distribution is typical of scabies in a healthy adult?","choices":["Back, neck, shoulders and waist as linear excoriations, with post-inflammatory pigmentation","Interdigital webs, sides of fingers, volar wrists, elbows, axillae and genitalia, sparing head and neck","Scalp and hairline first, then spreading down the neck and onto the upper back over weeks","Ankles and wrists first, then spreading centripetally to involve the palms and the soles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The areolae are also involved in women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are maculae caeruleae and which infestation produces them?","choices":["Linear excoriations on the back, neck, shoulders and waist with post-inflammatory pigmentation, in body lice","Two millimetre erythematous macules or papules with scaling and excoriation, seen in head lice","A central violaceous area ringed by blanched skin and then by asymmetric erythema, in spider bites","Slate-grey to bluish irregular macules about 1 cm across representing haemorrhage, seen in pubic lice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside papular urticaria at the feeding sites, commonly periumbilical. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How is hyperkeratotic scabies in an immunosuppressed patient treated?","choices":["Topical permethrin applied overnight on a single occasion with no repeat application at all","Oral albendazole 400 mg daily for three days with no topical therapy given alongside it","Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for ten to fourteen days as monotherapy","Ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses, with topical permethrin every three days to weekly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that combination is used when individual therapy fails. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which organism causes scabies and how is it usually transmitted?","choices":["Pediculus humanus capitis, by direct head-to-head contact between two individuals","Cimex lectularius, by spread in the clothing and baggage of travellers and visitors","Borrelia burgdorferi, by the blood meal of a tick that has fed on a reservoir animal","Sarcoptes scabiei variety hominis, by close physical contact for fifteen to twenty minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It may also be acquired from the bedding or underclothing of an infested person. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How should a honeybee sting be removed and why?","choices":["Apply ice for twenty minutes first, then remove it once the swelling has settled","Scrape it off with a card edge or dull knife held parallel to the skin, as fast as possible","Grasp it with fine forceps and pull it straight out with steady traction on the shaft","Leave it in place, since removing it releases the remaining venom into the tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the barbed ovipositor stays impaled and continues to pump venom. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the hallmark sign of a brown recluse bite?","choices":["The bull's eye sign — a lesion over 5 cm with central clearing and a darker punctate centre","The train-track sign — two parallel rows of purpura at the site of contact with the skin","The red, white and blue sign — a central violaceous area, a blanched rim, then large asymmetric erythema","The delta-wing jet sign — a dense dark head with the body, eggs and burrow trailing behind it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reactions range from mild and local to severe ulcerative necrosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which physical methods are described for head lice, and what is their limitation?","choices":["Weekly application of an occlusive agent such as mayonnaise or mineral oil to smother the live lice and eggs","Head shaving, or combing nits out after two minutes of hair moisturiser every few days; effective but slow and painful","Head shaving alone, which is completely curative and requires no further treatment at all","Daily hot air drying of the scalp, which kills both the live lice and the attached eggs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they need adjuvant therapy alongside them. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which specific physical measure is used in caterpillar exposure?","choices":["Stripping the hairs off the skin with adhesive tape","Scraping the site with a card edge held parallel to the skin","Applying blue or black ink and looking for a zigzag line","Combing the area after applying a hair moisturiser for two minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside symptomatic therapy and antivenom for certain categories. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What causes cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch?","choices":["Penetration of the stratum corneum by a mite that tunnels through it as it moves","Penetration of the skin by the cercarial larval form of certain parasitic flatworms","Penetration of the skin by the larva of an animal hookworm from contaminated soil","Penetration of the skin by an adult female flea that then lays her eggs within it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the cercaria passes from an intermediate host such as a snail to another host. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Describe the classic lesion of cutaneous larva migrans.","choices":["A large expanding round lesion over 5 cm with central clearing at the site of a bite","Erythematous papules in linear streaks after brushing against a caterpillar's hairs","An erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine trail advancing 2 to 3 cm each day","A thin thread-like linear or J-shaped tunnel 1 to 10 mm long in the interdigital webs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — intensely pruritic and painful, lasting two to eight weeks, with rare systemic symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How do primary and secondary skin lesions differ in the depth they affect?","choices":["Primary lesions affect the epidermis and superficial dermis; secondary lesions infiltrate the dermis or subcutaneous tissue","Primary lesions infiltrate the dermis and subcutaneous tissue; secondary lesions affect only the superficial epidermis","Primary lesions arise from infection while secondary lesions arise from an immune reaction to that infection","Primary lesions appear first in time while secondary lesions appear only after treatment has been started"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their combination determines the diagnostic category, also called the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What draws bedbugs out to feed, and where do they hide during the day?","choices":["Warmth and carbon dioxide draw them out; they hide in cracks and crevices of headboards and picture frames","Light and movement draw them out; they hide in the seams of clothing worn close to the skin","Perspiration and body odour draw them out; they burrow into the stratum corneum of the skin","Blood on the skin surface draws them out; they live permanently attached to the hair shaft"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they also hide behind loose wallpaper and in other dark places. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why is facility-associated scabies a particular concern?","choices":["Residents are homeless or displaced, in whom infestation persists through unwashed clothing","Residents travel frequently, carrying the organism between facilities in luggage and bedding","Residents are elderly and immunosuppressed, and a hospital epidemic can follow their admission","Residents are children under twelve, in whom the infestation spreads through shared headgear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is difficult to eradicate once healthcare workers are infested. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How often do bedbugs need a blood meal, and how long can they survive without one?","choices":["Every one to two days, and they die within a week without one","Once a month, and they can survive for up to three months","Twice daily, and they cannot survive more than forty-eight hours","Every five to ten days, but they can survive for up to a year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why an unoccupied room does not clear an infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the prognosis and treatment of a hobo spider bite?","choices":["Wounds require surgical reconstruction and healing takes months; treatment is operative","Wounds resolve within hours; treatment is calcium gluconate with muscle relaxants","Wounds resolve within days; treatment is a ten-day course of oral doxycycline","Wounds heal within several weeks and headaches may last a week; treatment is supportive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which diseases do rat fleas and cat fleas transmit?","choices":["Rat fleas transmit bubonic plague; cat fleas are a vector for bubonic plague and endemic typhus","Rat fleas transmit Lyme disease; cat fleas are a vector for Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Rat fleas transmit tungiasis; cat fleas are a vector for cutaneous larva migrans","Rat fleas transmit endemic typhus only; cat fleas transmit no human disease at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both belong to the family Pulicidae. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When is serological testing for Lyme disease most useful?","choices":["In patients who do not live in an endemic region and present with signs possibly consistent with it","In every patient who reports a tick bite, regardless of whether any rash has developed","In patients with erythema migrans, in whom the result determines whether to treat","In patients from endemic regions, in whom a negative result excludes the diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a patient with erythema migrans is diagnosed and treated clinically. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"}]};
